## Build

```sh
make build            # the production bundle, dist/server.js
make run              # start it on :{{ .Port }}
make run-dist         # the bundle, from an empty directory
```

`make run` uses `npm start`, which passes flags the server needs; running
`node server.ts` directly fails in ways that look like a code problem.

`make run-dist` is the one worth knowing about. The image copies
`dist/server.js` and nothing else — no `node_modules`, no source — so
anything read from disk at runtime resolves in a repo checkout and 500s
in the container. This reproduces the image's layout locally.

## Testing

```sh
make test             # typecheck, then the tests. What CI runs.
```

Add a service's own setup — a database, a seed step, extra env — to
`Makefile.local`. It is not generated, so it survives a regenerate.

## Calling this service

```ts
import { createClient } from './clients/ts'

const client = createClient({
  baseUrl: 'https://{{ .Name }}',
  name: 'your-service-name',
})
```

`name` is YOUR service's name, not the one being called: it goes out as
`Client-Name`, so whoever operates the service you call can tell who is
calling. It is required for that reason.

That gives a 5s timeout per attempt, one retry, `Retry-After` obeyed up
to 30s, and no circuit breaker. Pass `policy: exponentialRetry` or
`breaker: new Breaker(5, 30_000)` when a call wants something else.

### Testing a service that calls this one

`fetch` replaces the network, under the retries - so a test can answer
from memory and still exercise the timeout, the retries and the
breaker:

```ts
createClient({ baseUrl, name: 'your-service-name',
               fetch: async () => new Response('{}', { status: 200 }) })
```

Build the client where the service starts and pass it to whatever
needs it, rather than exporting one: a test can then hand over its own
without patching globals.

### Tracing

`createResilientFetch` returns a `fetch` and hey-api takes one, so a
tracing wrapper composes around it:

```ts
const resilient = createResilientFetch({ name: 'your-service-name' })
createClient({ baseUrl, name: 'your-service-name',
               fetch: (input, init) => withSpan(() => resilient(input, init)) })
```

Wrapping outside gives one span for the logical call; wrapping inside
gives one per attempt.

The `fetch` here is hey-api's, which sits over the retries, while the
`fetch` in `createClient`'s own options sits under them. Same word,
either side of the retry loop, depending on which object you put it
in. Passing hey-api's replaces the resilient fetch rather than
composing with it, which is why the example calls it rather than
dropping it.

## Deploy

CI builds and pushes an image on every push to `main`;
argocd-image-updater rolls it out. Nothing to run by hand.

```sh
homelabctl check deploy   # manifests and config
homelabctl status         # what Argo made of it
homelabctl diff           # what would change in the GitOps repo
```

Manifests are generated from `config.yaml` by `homelabctl render` —
change the config, not `deploy/`.
